A range is checked against the volume before it is freed

ext4_balloc_free_blocks turned its arguments into a first and last group
id by division and then walked from one to the other. Nothing established
that the last group exists. A range running past the end of the medium --
from a corrupt extent, or an extent tree walked with a bad length -- was
therefore walked as if those groups were there: each iteration credited
the superblock a bitmap block's worth of free space and wrote the same
credit through the descriptor table's address arithmetic, into whatever
that pointed at.

The result is a descriptor claiming more free blocks than its group holds,
which is the state an 8 GB stick arrived in after copying files: three
groups impossible, the cached total climbing past the size of the volume,
and allocation collapsing to eight-block runs because the allocator was
scanning groups for blocks that do not exist.

Reproduced by planting one extent that overhangs the end of the volume and
removing the file: before this, the last group ends up claiming more than
it holds; after it, the in-range blocks are still freed and the accounting
stays exact.

Refused rather than clamped. Blocks outside the volume were never this
inode's to free, so freeing the part of the range that happens to land is
not a more forgiving version of the same call -- it is a different one, and
it would hide the corrupt extent that produced the range.

--- a/src/ext4_balloc.c
+++ b/src/ext4_balloc.c
@@ -238,6 +238,35 @@
 	struct ext4_fs *fs = inode_ref->fs;
 	struct ext4_sblock *sb = &fs->sb;
 
+	/*
+	 * Refuse a range that is not wholly inside the volume.
+	 *
+	 * The group ids below are a division, and the loop then walks from the
+	 * first to the last. Nothing here checked that the last one exists, so
+	 * a range running past the end of the medium -- from a corrupt extent,
+	 * or an extent tree walked with a bad length -- walked group ids that
+	 * do not, crediting the superblock a bitmap block's worth of free space
+	 * per iteration and writing the credit into whatever the descriptor
+	 * table's address arithmetic pointed at.
+	 *
+	 * Observed on an 8 GB volume: freeing one extent that overhung the end
+	 * left the last group claiming more free blocks than the group holds,
+	 * which is the shape a field volume arrived in. The blocks in such a
+	 * range were never ours to free, so the honest answer is to refuse the
+	 * call rather than to clamp it and free the part that happens to land.
+	 */
+	uint64_t blocks_cnt = ext4_sb_get_blocks_cnt(sb);
+	if (count == 0)
+		return EOK;
+	if (first < ext4_get32(sb, first_data_block) ||
+	    first >= blocks_cnt || (uint64_t)count > blocks_cnt - first) {
+		ext4_dbg(DEBUG_BALLOC,
+			 DBG_ERROR "refusing to free %" PRIu32 " block(s) at %"
+			 PRIu64 ": past the end of a %" PRIu64 "-block volume\n",
+			 count, (uint64_t)first, blocks_cnt);
+		return EINVAL;
+	}
+
 	/* Compute indexes */
 	uint32_t bg_first = ext4_balloc_get_bgid_of_block(sb, first);
 
